## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design runtime policy enforcement and safe prompt routing under latency and drift.

## Key Concepts
- Runtime policy loading
- Injection detection and redaction
- Token budgeting and trimming
- Auditable decision logs

## Code Example
```javascript
// Minimal prototype sketch
const fetch = require('node-fetch');
async function buildPrompt(basePrompt){
  const policy = await fetch('http://localhost:8000/policy').then(r=>r.json());
  // injection guard
  let p = basePrompt.replace(/{{.*?}}/g, '');
  // secrets redaction
  p = p.replace(/(API_KEY|SECRET|PASSWORD)=[^\s]+/g, '[REDACTED]');
  // token cap (pseudo)
  const max = policy.maxTokens ?? 512; 
  return p.length > max ? p.slice(0, max) : p;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test resilience to policy drift over time?
- What instrumentation would you add to monitor latency and false positives in production?

## Why This Is Asked
Aims to gauge handling of ambiguous prompts with simple, maintainable logic—crucial for multi-tenant data workflows without heavy ML.

## Key Concepts
- Lightweight keyword-based intent detection
- Ambiguity handling with clarifying questions
- Structured 2-step plans and module payloads
- Lightweight testing with sample prompts

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo JS prototype for disambiguation harness
function detectIntents(text){
  const intents = {
    time: /(last|recent|past|last month)/i,
    region: /(region|country|geo)/i,
    users: /(users|accounts|people)/i
  };
  const hits = Object.keys(intents).filter(k => intents[k].test(text));
  return hits;
}

function disambiguate(text){
  const intents = detectIntents(text);
  if (intents.length > 1){
    return {ambiguous: true, options: ["summarize by region", "summarize by time"]};
  }
  if (intents.length === 1){
    return {ambiguous: false, plan: ["Fetch data", "Summarize"], payload: {module: intents[0]}};
  }
  return {ambiguous: false, plan: ["Interpret", "Respond"], payload: {module: 'default'}};
}

console.log(disambiguate("summarize recent activity"));
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you scale the heuristic to handle multilingual prompts?
- What tests would you add to prevent regression when adding new intents?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design stable prompts across models and languages with guardrails.

## Key Concepts
- Prompt contracts, per-model templates, and intent extraction
- Drift testing and edge-case handling for ambiguity
- Guardrails and multilingual normalization
- Minimal prototype demonstration

## Code Example
```javascript
// Minimal prototype sketch
function routePrompt(userInput, context) {
  const templates = {
    en: { default: 'Template_EN' },
    fr: { default: 'Template_FR' }
  };
  const model = context.model || 'default';
  const lang = context.lang || 'en';
  const chosen = (templates[lang] && templates[lang].default) || templates['en'].default;
  const finalPrompt = chosen + '\n' + userInput;
  return { template: chosen, finalPrompt, guardrail: 'none' };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test cross-language behavior and detect prompt drift?
- How would you version templates and roll them out safely?

## Why This Is Asked
Assess ability to optimize prompts for cost and speed, exercising token budgeting and simple NLP heuristics in prompt engineering.

## Key Concepts
- Token counting and budgets
- Intent preservation
- Heuristic trimming

## Code Example
```javascript
// Minimal compress
function compress(p){
  // naive: drop time qualifiers and rephrase
  let q = p
    .replace(/in the last \d+ months?/i, "")
    .replace(/and export\s+their\s+emails\s+to\s+a\s+CSV\s+file/gi, "export emails to CSV");
  return q.trim();
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you evaluate compression ratio vs fidelity?
- How would you handle languages other than English?
